# Divisi - the string ensemble: twelve top oscillators divided down in
# octaves, keys gated into six registers (violin, viola, cello, contrabass,
# choir and brass), each a section of players, seated on a stage, moved by a
# slow motion, then a chorus that can be new or old, and a phaser.
#
# 64 patches in seven families, plainest first: strings (sections as a
# player hears them, from the classic machine to sixteen players), stage
# (the sections seated, with room and split), low, choir, brass, motion (a
# slow shape across the sections, the synced ones for songs) and worn (the
# chorus aged, some on tape). A few in each family are the classic machine
# with one player and no stage. Pads play `pad`, seated patches `stage`,
# brass `chord` and motion patches `held`, one chord for sixteen seconds.
#
# Inserts: halls and plates where strings and choirs always had them, a
# wooden body on the chamber and solo patches, tape on some of the worn
# ones. They sit within the effect-level rule; the patch's volume levels the
# patch with them on.
#
# Levels: every patch is levelled by `volume` to about -29.5 on `loud`, one
# note at its own note, with its inserts. A chord of four reads about 6 dB
# louder. Slow patches are levelled with a quick attack and swell patches
# with the motion off (the top of the breath), so they hold the same level
# as the rest once they get there. Violin and viola peak about 15 dB over
# their rms, so a big chord at full velocity can still peak near full scale;
# the master's limiter takes that.
#
# Every patch but Init carries eight named macros. Destinations are numbered
# as in Divisi.h's MacroDest: #1 violin, #2 viola, #3 cello, #4 contrabass,
# #5 choir, #6 vowel, #7 brass, #8 sweep, #9 brass decay, #10 blat, #11 tone,
# #12 attack, #13 release, #14 ensemble, #15 speed, #16 phaser, #17 rate,
# #18 feedback, #19 velocity, #20 volume, #21 pitch (24 semitones a unit),
# #22 motion balance, #23 motion vowel, #24 motion tone, #25 motion
# ensemble, #26 motion rate, #27 age, #28 players, #29 drift, #30 vibrato, #31 vibrate, #32 scatter,
# #33 stage, #34 room, #35 split.
#
# The set is the same on every patch: Bright, Slow, Lush, Voices, Brass,
# Phase, Motion and Age. Slow is the attack and release together. A macro
# whose knob has no room left goes the other way under the opposite name
# (Dark, Quick, Drier, Vowel, Blat or Smooth, Still, Calm, Newer). A patch
# with no motion has Low (the contrabass) or Light in Motion's place. Where a
# macro at full moves the level by more than 1.2 dB, a volume route takes it
# back out.
#
# Values are in each parameter's own units; `audition params Divisi` lists
# them. A patch names only what it changes.

machine Divisi
role    pad

# --- strings ------------------------------------------------------------------

patch Init family=strings note=60 range=36..84

patch "Full Strings" family=strings note=60 range=36..84
  # The classic machine: violins, violas and cellos through the chorus.
  set macro1 "Bright"
  set macro2 "Slow"
  set macro3 "Drier"
  set macro4 "Voices"
  set macro5 "Brass"
  set macro6 "Phase"
  set macro7 "Low"
  set macro8 "Age"
